WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.743 --> 00:00:03.257
- [Narrator] Salmon sushi isn't Japanese.

00:00:03.257 --> 00:00:04.765
It's Norwegian.

00:00:04.765 --> 00:00:06.514
- [Woman] Huh?

00:00:06.514 --> 00:00:08.173
Mm, sushi.

00:00:08.173 --> 00:00:09.366
- [Narrator] Okay, here's the story.

00:00:09.366 --> 00:00:14.341
Until about 1995, raw salmon
was not consumed in Japan

00:00:14.341 --> 00:00:16.821
and this was for one big reason.

00:00:16.821 --> 00:00:21.152
Pacific salmon has parasites,
so no salmon sushi,

00:00:21.152 --> 00:00:23.541
sashimi, no spicy salmon, nothing.

00:00:23.541 --> 00:00:24.374
- [Woman] What?

00:00:24.374 --> 00:00:25.207
No way.
- But, obviously

00:00:25.207 --> 00:00:28.047
that all changed thanks
in part to this guy.

00:00:28.047 --> 00:00:31.064
- [Bjorn] Yes, my name
is Bjørn Eirik Olsen.

00:00:31.064 --> 00:00:32.838
- [Narrator] Norway had a problem.

00:00:32.838 --> 00:00:36.026
They had a lot of salmon
and too small a market.

00:00:36.026 --> 00:00:38.917
Looking east, they set
their sights on Japan,

00:00:38.917 --> 00:00:41.404
a country that loves raw fish so much,

00:00:41.404 --> 00:00:45.107
it's willing to pay five times
the price for sushi-grade.

00:00:45.107 --> 00:00:49.197
So, Norway formed a
committee and hired Bjørn.

00:00:49.197 --> 00:00:53.364
- [Bjorn] From 1986 to 1991,
I worked for Project Japan.

00:00:56.178 --> 00:00:58.389
- [Narrator] Their job was to
convince Japanese consumers

00:00:58.389 --> 00:01:01.953
that Atlantic salmon
was safe to consume raw,

00:01:01.953 --> 00:01:03.810
and this was hard.

00:01:03.810 --> 00:01:05.321
Just take, for example, pork.

00:01:05.321 --> 00:01:07.520
If some guy from Norway
came here and said,

00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:09.355
here, eat our pork meat raw.

00:01:09.355 --> 00:01:10.914
It's safe, it's really yummy.

00:01:10.914 --> 00:01:13.303
We'd be like man, you're crazy.

00:01:13.303 --> 00:01:15.703
- [Bjørn] It was basically
a matter of perception,

00:01:15.703 --> 00:01:19.021
so we had to change the
whole country's opinion.

00:01:19.021 --> 00:01:20.485
- [Narrator] Bjørn and
his team got to work.

00:01:20.485 --> 00:01:22.195
They made the first-ever salmon rolls

00:01:22.195 --> 00:01:24.234
and served them to businessmen in Japan,

00:01:24.234 --> 00:01:28.063
which they didn't like for a long time.

00:01:28.063 --> 00:01:29.813
- [Bjørn] It took actually 10 years

00:01:29.813 --> 00:01:32.748
to get the proper
breakthrough in the market.

00:01:32.748 --> 00:01:33.763
- [Narrator] So, there it is.

00:01:33.763 --> 00:01:36.674
Salmon sushi didn't exist before 1995.

00:01:36.674 --> 00:01:40.261
Bjørn, on behalf of the
world, we thank you.

00:01:40.261 --> 00:01:44.011
(gentle instrumental music)

